  "notes": [
    {
      "id": "objects",
      "text": "1. KEEP THE SAME OBJECTS\nThis sparse 3D sample contains a branched network, a separate line and one isolated voxel. Label first; both measurements use the same filtered labels. The minimum volume of 1 keeps the isolate. Try 2 to remove it from both branches.",
      "position": [
        0,
        -260
      ],
      "width": 620
    },
    {
      "id": "identity",
      "text": "2. PRESERVE LABEL IDENTITY\nSkeletonize Labels thins each original label independently. Analyze Skeleton per Label receives the original labels AND that skeleton. Its main table has one row per original label; the Skeleton components output lists any disconnected pieces within that label.",
      "position": [
        1450,
        -260
      ],
      "width": 660
    },
    {
      "id": "joins",
      "text": "3. JOIN AND INSPECT\nJoin morphology and skeleton summaries by label_id for this one 3D image. Across images or timepoints, also include image and leading-axis identity. Isolated nodes are degree-zero skeleton voxels; they are not all separate objects. Keep raw columns.",
      "position": [
        2670,
        -260
      ],
      "width": 640
    },
    {
      "id": "calibration",
      "text": "4. DESCRIPTIVE EXAMPLE\nSynthetic spacing is 0.45 micrometer in Z, Y and X. Volume and skeleton length use that same calibration. Inspect all three points, including the zero-length isolate. This is a fixture, not evidence of biological independence or valid segmentation of acquired images.",
      "position": [
        3820,
        -260
      ],
      "width": 650
    }
